 First, I will analyze the problem description. The user wants me to implement a hierarchical design where the original full_module is broken down into a submodule called TopModule, which will be instantiated three times. The original module uses a 3-bit register q that gets updated based on a control signal L and input r. The key insight is that q behaves like three separate flip-flops, each with their own update logic.

Looking at the original code, q[2:0] is updated in a single always block on the clock edge. When L is high, q gets loaded directly from r. Otherwise, it performs a bitwise transformation: {q[1] ^ q[2], q[0], q[2]}. This suggests that each bit of q is updated independently, but the transformation depends on the entire q value, not just individual bits.

For the hierarchical design, I need to break this into three separate flip-flops, each with their own control logic. The transformation logic {q[1] ^ q[2], q[0], q[2]} can be decomposed into three independent operations: q[0] becomes q[0] XOR q[2], q[1] becomes q[1] XOR q[2], and q[2] remains unchanged. This decomposition allows us to create three identical flip-flop submodules, each handling one bit of the original q.

The control signal L needs to be shared across all three flip-flops since it's a global control for the entire module. When L is high, all three flip-flops load from r simultaneously. Otherwise, each flip-flop follows its specific transformation rule based on the current q value.

The design choice to use three identical submodules simplifies the hierarchy and ensures symmetry. Each submodule handles one bit position, making the design scalable and easier to verify. The shared L signal ensures consistent control across all flip-flops.

